FINDING (task_bug, low severity, breaks_ac:false): StoreLogic::get_stores'
new `fastest` branch (~line 217) omits the `orderBy('id')` tie-break that
the sibling `nearest`/`top_rated` branches in the SAME file carry (with an
explicit NEARS-628-class comment: "id tie-break: equal averages/same-tower
stores are common — without it offset pagination can duplicate/drop rows
across pages"). Repro of the risk (not observed as an actual duplicate/drop
here only because the seeded QA data's ties (10,10 / 15,15 / 20,20 / 30,30 /
45,45 min) happen not to straddle the limit=12 page boundary — verified
stable across 3 repeated curl calls above, position 12 = the sole min-35
store). Recommend adding `->orderBy('id')` to the `fastest` `->when()` clause
to match the established pattern and remove the latent risk for other data
shapes.
